  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-11+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg\u2061(z)\u2248$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/assets/d3b5558c0268843618c52c588ffcf1c8012189041b17d52fadd6fe74c3526700.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg\u2061(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan\u2061\\alpha  & =\\frac{8}{11} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\u2061(\\frac{8}{11}) \\\\  & =0.628\\text{\u00a0}796\\text{\u00a0}\u2026\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg\u2061(z)=\\pi -0.628\\text{\u00a0}796\\text{\u00a0}\u2026\u22482.51,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
